Exactly. [ka broda] really doesn't have that much to do with "-ness" at
all, by itself. What [ka broda] means exactly, really depends on the selbri
relating it.
[xy. silnyselvu'i] -> [xy. silna se vrusi] -> [xy. se vrusi lo ka silna] ->
[lo ka silna cu vrusi xy.] "X tastes salty/like salt."
From what I gather about [ka] and the way it's used, it makes a secondary
claim. In this case, the secondary claim is that [xy] *is* a silna1, in a
way. In particular, it's silna1 such that it *tastes* like silna1 tastes,
i.e. salty.

Making up gismu isn't done lightly, and making up zi'evla/fu'ivla for
things that have short lujvo equivalents is a "waste" (hardly, considering
zi'evla-space is unlimited) of zi'evla-space.

All in all, [broda se vrusi] produces a [x1 tastes like <lo broda>] type of
selbri; I'd say that's the best solution, when such a broda exists.
Otherwise, we have to resort to fu'ivla.

mu'o mi'e la tsani

